84 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 49 0 R
/E (To the best of our survey, few frameworks combine dynamic DRL-based assignment, explicit risk awareness, workload balancing, comparison with an optimisation baseline and per-decision explanation. SmartRoute AI is designed to fill this gap.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
85 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 49 0 R
/T (II. LITERATURE SURVEY)
/E (II. LITERATURE SURVEY)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
86 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 49 0 R
/E (Representative publications on the vehicle routing problem, optimal assignment, reinforcement learning for dispatch, and explainable and safe reinforcement learning are summarised below, together with their limitations and their relation to the proposed work.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
87 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 49 0 R
/E (Dantzig and Ramser [15] introduced the truck dispatching problem, which became the vehicle routing problem, and it remains the foundation of delivery optimisation. Boysen et al. [1] surveyed last-mile delivery concepts from an operational research view and described the many variants and the pressure created by customer time expectations. These works define the problem but are based on mathematical optimisation that is usually solved offline for known demand.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
88 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 49 0 R
/E (Kuhn [2] proposed the Hungarian method, which solves the one-to-one assignment problem optimally for a given cost matrix. It is a natural baseline, and it is used in SmartRoute AI for this purpose. Its limitation is that it optimises one snapshot of costs and does not learn how present assignments affect future orders, workload or risk.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
89 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 49 0 R
/E (Mnih et al. [3] showed that deep Q-networks can learn control policies directly from experience, and Schulman et al. [4] proposed Proximal Policy Optimization, a stable policy-gradient method. Sutton and Barto [16] give the standard theory of reinforcement learning. These methods supply the learning machinery for the DRL agent, but they do not address delivery assignment on their own.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
90 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 49 0 R
/E (Nazari et al. [5] and Kool et al. [6] applied neural networks and reinforcement learning to vehicle routing and obtained good routes without hand-designed heuristics. Their focus is route construction rather than the continuous assignment of arriving orders to partners, and they do not model risk or explanation.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
91 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 49 0 R
/E (In ride-hailing, Xu et al. [7] studied large-scale order dispatch by combining learning and planning, and Lin et al. [8] used multi-agent deep reinforcement learning for fleet management. These works are the closest to the dynamic assignment setting and show that learned dispatch can improve platform-level efficiency. They aim at efficiency measures such as response and utilisation and offer little explanation of individual decisions.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
