273 0 obj
<<
/Title (Last-mile delivery is the final stage of the logistics chain, in which goods travel from a local hub to the customer. It is widely regarded as the most costly and the most difficult stage, because it involves many small orders, strict time expectations and an unpredictable road environment [1]. A central operational decision is the assignment problem: when an order arrives, which available delivery partner should serve it? A poor choice increases travel distance, delays the delivery and overloads some partners while others stay idle.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 221 0 R
/Next 314 0 R
>>
endobj
274 0 obj
<<
/Title (Delivery assignment must account for traffic congestion, delivery deadlines, partner availability, distance and operational risk at the same time. Static or rule-based methods such as nearest-partner assignment do not adapt well when conditions change, and learned policies are often treated as black boxes, which makes it difficult for dispatchers to trust or audit their choices. A method is needed that adapts to real-time conditions, takes risk into account and can justify each decision.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 221 0 R
/Prev 315 0 R
>>
endobj
